---
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import TopicClusterNav from '../components/TopicClusterNav.astro';

const pageTitle = 'Dry Creek Bed: Measure Stone and Plan the Water Route';
const metaDescription = 'Estimate stone and geotextile fabric for a decorative dry creek bed or drainage swale, and separate the quantity calculation from hydraulic channel design.';

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'How do I measure width for a curved dry creek bed with sloped banks?',
    a: 'Follow the cross-sectional path of the stone rather than just top-down width. Measure along the flat bottom plus both sloped sides where stone will sit, then multiply by section length.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a universal 60/30/10 rock ratio for dry creek beds?',
    a: 'No. Stone gradation depends on flow velocity, slope steepness, soil beneath the bed, and outlet conditions. Use larger river cobbles or engineered riprap where active stormwater scours lighter pebbles.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Should geotextile fabric go under dry creek rock?',
    a: 'Permeable separation geotextile helps keep subgrade soil from mixing into stone linings, especially when sized and overlapped for the site. It cannot size the channel or replace a proper stormwater outlet.'
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={metaDescription} canonicalPath="/dry-creek-bed">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'Article',
      headline: 'Dry Creek Bed: Measure the Stone Lining',
      description: metaDescription,
      mainEntityOfPage: 'https://rockcoveragecalculator.com/dry-creek-bed',
      inLanguage: 'en',
      dateModified: '2026-11-25'
    })} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      mainEntity: faqItems.map(item => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: item.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a }
      }))
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <Header />
  <main id="main-content" class="container practical-guide">
    <Breadcrumbs />
    <h1>Dry Creek Bed: Measure the Stone Lining</h1>
    <p class="intro">Measure the bed bottom and sloping banks to estimate a stone lining. Establish the water route and outlet before treating a decorative creek-bed quantity as part of a drainage project.</p>

    <section class="creek-estimator" aria-labelledby="estimator-heading">
      <div class="estimator-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Swale &amp; creek lining calculator</p>
          <h2 id="estimator-heading">Estimate channel stone &amp; separator fabric</h2>
        </div>
        <p>Include the bottom width plus both lined bank widths so side slopes are not under-ordered.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="estimator-grid">
        <div class="estimator-inputs">
          <div class="field-row">
            <div class="input-field"><label for="dcb-length">Channel run length (ft)</label><input id="dcb-length" type="number" value="20" min="1" step="any" inputmode="decimal" /></div>
            <div class="input-field"><label for="dcb-bottom">Bed bottom width (ft)</label><input id="dcb-bottom" type="number" value="1.5" min="0.1" step="any" inputmode="decimal" /></div>
          </div>
          <div class="field-row">
            <div class="input-field"><label for="dcb-bank">Each lined side slope (ft)</label><input id="dcb-bank" type="number" value="0.75" min="0" step="any" inputmode="decimal" /></div>
            <div class="input-field"><label for="dcb-depth">Stone lining thickness (in)</label><input id="dcb-depth" type="number" value="4" min="1" max="36" step="any" inputmode="decimal" /></div>
          </div>
          <div class="field-row">
            <div class="input-field"><label for="dcb-extra">Order allowance (%)</label><input id="dcb-extra" type="number" value="10" min="0" max="50" step="any" inputmode="decimal" /></div>
            <div class="input-field"><label for="dcb-density">Supplier weight (short tons/yd³)</label><input id="dcb-density" type="number" value="1.35" min="0.4" max="3" step="0.01" inputmode="decimal" /></div>
          </div>
          <div class="input-field"><label for="dcb-overlap">Fabric edge tuck &amp; seam allowance (%)</label><input id="dcb-overlap" type="number" value="15" min="0" max="60" step="any" inputmode="decimal" /></div>
          <p id="dcb-error" class="dcb-error" role="alert" hidden></p>
        </div>
        <div class="estimator-results" aria-live="polite">
          <div class="res-hero"><span id="dcb-order-yd">0.81 yd³</span><small id="dcb-tons">≈ 1.10 short tons (with 10% allowance)</small></div>
          <dl>
            <div><dt>Total lined width</dt><dd id="dcb-width">3.00 ft</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Lined surface area</dt><dd id="dcb-area">60 sq ft</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Net installed stone</dt><dd id="dcb-net-yd">0.74 yd³</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Geotextile fabric (incl. overlap)</dt><dd id="dcb-fabric">69 sq ft</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <figure class="creek-figure">
      <img src="/river-rock-measuring-guide.svg" width="900" height="520" loading="lazy" alt="Diagram comparing flat bed length times width against a dry creek cross section whose lined stone width spans the channel bottom and both side slopes." />
      <figcaption>Measure the wetted perimeter across the bed bottom and both lined side slopes—horizontal plan width alone underestimates curved or sloped banks.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Start with the route and outlet</h2>
    <p>A decorative dry creek feature and a channel carrying stormwater can look alike while doing different jobs. Identify where water enters and where it can leave before choosing stone. Observe the route during a safe rain from a safe location. Do not assume that a shallow stone strip will handle a roof, road or hillside catchment.</p>
    <p>Where the work changes drainage, affects a stream or wetland, crosses utilities, or could send runoff toward a building or neighboring land, establish the required design and permissions before excavation. This guide measures a proposed stone lining; it does not size a channel for a design storm.</p>

    <h2>Measure the surface that stone will cover</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Divide a curved route into short sections with reasonably consistent width and depth.</li>
      <li>Measure the width of the stone-lined surface, including side slopes when they will receive stone. Horizontal plan width can understate the surface of a steep-sided channel.</li>
      <li>Choose the lining thickness from the actual product and project detail. A stone’s advertised size is not automatically its installed layer depth.</li>
      <li>Calculate each section and add the volumes. Calculate separate boulder, bedding or pipe-trench quantities when applicable.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>A quantity example</h2>
    <p>A decorative section 20 ft long and 3 ft across its measured lining surface has 60 sq ft. At an assumed 4-inch lining thickness, 60 × 4 ÷ 324 = 0.74 cubic yard. A chosen 10% order allowance makes 0.81 cubic yard. At an example loose weight of 1.35 short tons per yard, that is about 1.10 tons. None of those example values establishes the necessary stone size, channel capacity or erosion resistance.</p>

    <h2>Choose stone by its function</h2>
    <p>Small river stone can provide a decorative finish; larger cobbles or specified riprap may be part of a designed erosion-protection detail. There is no universal 60/30/10 stone blend or one channel shape suitable for every site. Water depth, velocity, slope, soil, bends and the outlet affect material selection and stability. Use the specified filter, bedding and stone gradation where the project requires them.</p>

    <h2>Separate layers and displacement</h2>
    <p>Do not count a complete excavation as solid stone if it also contains pipe, bedding, top cover or substantial embedded boulders. Use the <a href="/drain-rock-calculator">drain rock calculator</a> for a pipe trench. Use the <a href="/riprap-calculator">riprap quantity tool</a> only after the armor size and thickness have been selected.</p>

    <h2>Allow for inspection and maintenance</h2>
    <p>After installation and storms, look for displaced stone, sediment, blocked outlets, exposed fabric and erosion at transitions. A hose check does not demonstrate capacity for a major storm. Keep debris out of the flow path and revisit the design if erosion or overtopping continues.</p>
    <p>For ordering, confirm the actual loose weight and quote unit with the supplier. The <a href="https://dirtandgravel.psu.edu/general-resources/dglvr-materials-calculator/">Penn State materials calculator</a> explains the distinction between loose shipped quantity and finished dimensions. The <a href="/river-rock-calculator">river rock calculator</a> can convert your measured lining volume into an example order.</p>

    <section class="faq-block" aria-labelledby="creek-faq-heading">
      <h2 id="creek-faq-heading">Dry creek bed questions</h2>
      {faqItems.map(item => (
        <details><summary>{item.q}</summary><p>{item.a}</p></details>
      ))}
    </section>

    <TopicClusterNav />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  const ids = ['dcb-length', 'dcb-bottom', 'dcb-bank', 'dcb-depth', 'dcb-extra', 'dcb-density', 'dcb-overlap'] as const;
  const el = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as HTMLInputElement;
  const put = (id: string, text: string) => {
    const target = document.getElementById(id);
    if (target) target.textContent = text;
  };
  const fmt = (n: number, digits = 2) => new Intl.NumberFormat('en', { maximumFractionDigits: digits }).format(n);

  function updateCreekEstimator() {
    const err = document.getElementById('dcb-error') as HTMLElement;
    const vals = Object.fromEntries(ids.map(id => {
      const input = el(id);
      const n = Number(input.value);
      const ok = input.value.trim() !== '' && input.validity.valid && Number.isFinite(n);
      input.setAttribute('aria-invalid', String(!ok));
      return [id, { ok, n }];
    }));
    const valid = Object.values(vals).every(v => v.ok);
    err.hidden = valid;
    if (!valid) {
      err.textContent = 'Enter valid non-negative numbers for bank slope, extra, and fabric overlap, and positive numbers for run, bottom, depth, and density.';
      ['dcb-order-yd', 'dcb-tons', 'dcb-width', 'dcb-area', 'dcb-net-yd', 'dcb-fabric'].forEach(id => put(id, '—'));
      return;
    }
    const linedWidth = vals['dcb-bottom'].n + 2 * vals['dcb-bank'].n;
    const area = vals['dcb-length'].n * linedWidth;
    const netYds = area * vals['dcb-depth'].n / 324;
    const orderYds = netYds * (1 + vals['dcb-extra'].n / 100);
    const tons = orderYds * vals['dcb-density'].n;
    const fabricSqFt = Math.ceil(area * (1 + vals['dcb-overlap'].n / 100) - 1e-10);
    put('dcb-order-yd', fmt(orderYds) + ' yd³');
    put('dcb-tons', '≈ ' + fmt(tons) + ' short tons (with ' + fmt(vals['dcb-extra'].n) + '% allowance)');
    put('dcb-width', fmt(linedWidth) + ' ft');
    put('dcb-area', fmt(area, 1) + ' sq ft');
    put('dcb-net-yd', fmt(netYds) + ' yd³');
    put('dcb-fabric', fmt(fabricSqFt, 0) + ' sq ft');
  }

  ids.forEach(id => {
    const node = el(id);
    node?.addEventListener('input', updateCreekEstimator);
    node?.addEventListener('change', updateCreekEstimator);
  });
  updateCreekEstimator();
</script>

<style>
  .practical-guide { max-width: 960px; padding-block: var(--space-10); }
  h1 { font-size: var(--text-3xl); margin-block: var(--space-5); color: var(--text-primary); }
  h2 { font-size: var(--text-xl); margin-block: var(--space-7) var(--space-3); color: var(--text-primary); }
  p, li { line-height: 1.75; color: var(--text-secondary); }
  p, ul, ol { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  a { color: var(--accent-gold); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  ul, ol { padding-left: 1.4rem; }
  .creek-estimator { background: var(--bg-card); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: var(--space-6); margin: var(--space-6) 0 var(--space-8); }
  .estimator-head { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: end; gap: var(--space-4); margin-bottom: var(--space-5); }
  .estimator-head h2 { margin: 0; }
  .estimator-head > p { max-width: 30ch; margin: 0; font-size: var(--text-sm); }
  .eyebrow { margin: 0 0 .3rem; color: var(--accent-gold); font-size: var(--text-xs); font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .09em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .estimator-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.1fr .9fr; gap: var(--space-6); align-items: start; }
  .field-row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-3); }
  .input-field { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .input-field label { display: block; font-size: var(--text-xs); font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .input-field input { width: 100%; padding: .65rem .75rem; background: var(--bg-surface); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-md); color: var(--text-primary); }
  .input-field input[aria-invalid="true"] { border-color: var(--color-error); }
  .dcb-error { color: var(--color-error); font-size: var(--text-sm); margin: var(--space-2) 0 0; }
  .estimator-results { background: var(--bg-surface); border: 1px solid var(--accent-gold); border-radius: var(--radius-md); padding: var(--space-5); }
  .res-hero span { display: block; font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.8rem); font-weight: 800; color: var(--accent-gold); line-height: 1.1; }
  .res-hero small { display: block; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-top: 6px; font-size: var(--text-sm); }
  .estimator-results dl { margin: var(--space-4) 0 0; }
  .estimator-results dl > div { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--space-3); padding: 8px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); }
  .estimator-results dt { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: var(--text-sm); }
  .estimator-results dd { margin: 0; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .creek-figure { margin: var(--space-6) 0 var(--space-8); background: var(--bg-card); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: var(--space-4); }
  .creek-figure img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: var(--radius-md); background: #f8fafc; }
  .creek-figure figcaption { margin-top: var(--space-3); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .faq-block details { background: var(--bg-card); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-md); padding: var(--space-4); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .faq-block summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .faq-block details p { margin: var(--space-3) 0 0; }
  @media (max-width: 720px) { .estimator-grid, .estimator-head { grid-template-columns: 1fr; display: grid; } .field-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
